---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import CodeBlock from '../components/CodeBlock.astro';
import DownloadSizes from '../components/DownloadSizes.astro';
import SectionIntro from '../components/SectionIntro.astro';

const base = import.meta.env.BASE_URL;
const releaseUrl = 'https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli/releases';
const sourceExample = `git clone https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli.git
cd grr-cli
cargo build --release --locked
./target/release/grr`;
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Install grr"
  description="Install grr on macOS, Linux, and Windows (x64 and ARM) with Homebrew, winget, or crates.io, or build from source. Release binaries are zero-config."
>
  <section class="page-hero section">
    <div class="container page-hero-grid">
      <div class="reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">install grr</span>
        <h1>Pick the shortest path to your shell.</h1>
        <p>Prebuilt binaries are the easy default. If you want a package manager, a crates.io install, or a local build, the project documents each route below.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="page-hero-aside reveal">6 services with dedicated commands · 308 methods via <code>grr api</code> · 10 APIs</div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <div class="command-marquee" aria-label="Install commands">
    <div class="command-marquee-track">
      <div class="command-marquee-group">
        <span>brew tap debanjanbasu/homebrew</span>
        <span>brew trust debanjanbasu/homebrew</span>
        <span>brew install grr</span>
        <span>winget install debanjanbasu.grr</span>
        <span>cargo install grr-cli --locked</span>
      </div>
      <div class="command-marquee-group" aria-hidden="true">
        <span>brew tap debanjanbasu/homebrew</span>
        <span>brew trust debanjanbasu/homebrew</span>
        <span>brew install grr</span>
        <span>winget install debanjanbasu.grr</span>
        <span>cargo install grr-cli --locked</span>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>

  <section class="install-section section" aria-labelledby="channels-title">
    <div class="container">
      <SectionIntro id="channels-title" eyebrow="release channels" title="Install the way you already ship software." copy="The published binary is named grr, even though the crates.io package is grr-cli because the short crate name grr is already taken." />
      <div class="install-grid">
        <article class="install-card install-card-wide">
          <h2>Prebuilt binaries</h2>
          <p>Download the archive for your platform from GitHub Releases. The release workflow publishes Windows x64, Windows on ARM, Linux x64, and macOS ARM binaries with checksums. Every build is UPX-packed, so the archives stay small.</p>
          <DownloadSizes note="Compressed release archives for the current GitHub release. A dash means that platform is not published yet." />
          <a class="button button-primary" href={releaseUrl} target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Open GitHub Releases <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card">
          <h2>macOS + Linux</h2>
          <p>The repository’s Homebrew tap serves arm64 macOS and x86_64 Linux builds. Tap it, trust it, then install by formula name.</p>
          <p class="install-note">Since Homebrew 4.4, third-party taps are untrusted by default, so <code>brew trust</code> is required before Homebrew will install a formula from the tap.</p>
          <CodeBlock code={'brew tap debanjanbasu/homebrew\nbrew trust debanjanbasu/homebrew\nbrew install grr'} label="homebrew" />
        </article>
        <article class="install-card">
          <h2>Windows</h2>
          <p>The Windows package is available through winget, for both x64 and ARM64 machines. The published manifest is 0.4.0, matching the current GitHub release; 0.5.0 is in preparation and will land in the repository when its manifest review clears.</p>
          <CodeBlock code="winget install debanjanbasu.grr" label="winget" />
        </article>
        <article class="install-card">
          <h2>crates.io</h2>
          <p>The crate publishes as <code>grr-cli</code>; the installed executable is <code>grr</code>. The default CLI enables HTTP/3, so a crates.io source install needs nightly Rust and the <code>reqwest_unstable</code> cfg.</p>
          <CodeBlock code={'RUSTFLAGS="--cfg reqwest_unstable" cargo install grr-cli --locked'} label="cargo · unix" />
          <p class="install-note">PowerShell: set <code>$env:RUSTFLAGS='--cfg reqwest_unstable'</code> before running cargo. Library users can choose stable Rust with <code>default-features = false</code> and select only the service features they need.</p>
        </article>
        <article class="install-card">
          <h2>OAuth setup</h2>
          <p>Release binaries are zero-config: the OAuth client is compiled in at build time, so <code>grr auth login</code> works with no setup. Building from source? Run <code>grr auth setup</code> to create and store your own client, then log in.</p>
          <CodeBlock code={`grr auth login\ngrr gmail profile`} label="first run" />
        </article>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="source-section section" aria-labelledby="source-title">
    <div class="container source-grid">
      <div class="source-copy reveal">
        <span class="eyebrow">from source</span>
        <h2 id="source-title">Build the exact revision you want to run.</h2>
        <p>The repository pins Rust nightly in <code>rust-toolchain.toml</code>. A local clone uses the repository’s Cargo config, which sets the unstable reqwest cfg and the extra build-std settings needed by the CLI.</p>
        <p>The default CLI enables HTTP/3 through reqwest’s rustls/quinn path. The <code>grr_cli</code> library can use stable Rust with <code>default-features = false</code> and HTTP/2, while selecting services such as <code>gmail</code> as needed.</p>
        <a class="button button-secondary" href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli#development" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Read development notes <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a>
      </div>
      <CodeBlock code={sourceExample} label="local clone" />
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="split-section section" aria-labelledby="next-title">
    <div class="container prose">
      <span class="eyebrow">next step</span>
      <h2 id="next-title">Bring your own client, only if you build.</h2>
      <p>Release binaries need none of this — just run <code>grr auth login</code>. If you build from source, <code>grr auth setup</code> creates a Google Cloud project, enables the APIs, makes a Desktop OAuth client, and stores the client ID for you.</p>
      <ul>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli/blob/main/docs/gcp-setup.md" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">Google Cloud setup guide ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href="https://github.com/debanjanbasu/grr-cli#60-second-quickstart" target="_blank" rel="noreferrer">60-second quickstart ↗</a></li>
        <li><a href={base}>Back to the overview →</a></li>
      </ul>
    </div>
  </section>
</BaseLayout>
